paper(bonded): prove truthful claim-signaling is a Nash equilibrium

Add a new subsection sec:claim-signaling-ic to sec:economic between
"Why This Defeats the Three Adversaries" and "Pricing the Bond",
closing the paper's headline incentive-compatibility claim with a
real folk-theorem argument rather than carrying it on faith:

- Stage-game payoff matrix (prisoner's dilemma) calibrated to the
  cleanup cost c of sec:cleanup-bound.
- Repeated-game lift via observable history (Merkle forest
  sec:attribution) + persistent identity (Anchor Protocol) +
  discount factor delta.
- Three-round graduated trigger strategy. Closed-form sustainability
  condition for general k. For k=3 with (c,d,p)=(3,4,0): bound is
  delta > delta*_{k=3} ~ 0.253, marginally above the grim-trigger
  bound 1/4. (Brief had this at 0.53 -- that was an arithmetic
  error in the brief; the cubic 3d(1+d+d^2)=1 crosses near 0.253.
  Fixed in proof.)
- Proposition 7.1 (Claim Signaling IC) with tight-bound clause.
- Failure-mode enumeration: what breaks when each premise is
  removed. Makes explicit that the Anchor Protocol is a
  *precondition* of the Bonded result, not an unrelated artifact --
  the audit caught that this dependency had been implicit.

Plus two connecting clauses:

1. Opening of sec:youle:a6 (cartel folk-theorem): justifies why the
   analysis uses grim trigger (cleanest closed form) while
   production uses graduated trigger (crashes are operationally
   indistinguishable from defection -- sec:crash-recovery). The two
   layers share one folk theorem with two punishment regimes.

2. Scope hedge on sec:youle:welfare's Pareto-dominance claim: pins
   it to "mature market with calibrated reputation history and
   sufficient insurer volume to support price discovery" with a
   forward ref to sec:pricing:bootstrap (label being created by
   sibling agent paper/pricing-bootstrap-threats-settlement --
   forward ref will resolve once that branch lands).

Mechanics:
- New theorem env: newtheorem{proposition}{Proposition}[section].
- New bibitem: aumann1974subjectivity (Subjectivity and Correlation
  in Randomized Strategies, 1974). The abstract already cited it
  but the bibitem was missing; this was previously rendering
  undefined.
- Build: pdflatex x 3, 40 pages, no math/font errors. Two LaTeX
  warnings remain: pre-existing sec:revocation cross-paper ref and
  the expected sec:pricing:bootstrap forward ref.

Constraints respected: no edits to s1, s2, abstract, Reader's Map,
or sec:pricing. No new figures. Builds clean against current main.


